The Python provider creates an application or library with ordinary Python or uv. Follow [Your first Python project](/docs/first-python-project/) for a guided run and test.

## Generated project

The starter declares Python 3.11 or newer in `pyproject.toml`, uses setuptools, and contains a `src/<name>/` package and a unittest test. Applications also include `__main__.py` and a console entry point. The starter test checks addition; the application prints `42`.

For generated projects, tasks add the absolute `src/` directory to the Python import path. Ordinary Python can therefore run and test the starter before installation. Installing the package with `python -m pip install -e .` makes its console entry point available in the selected environment. See the [Python packaging guide](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/guides/writing-pyproject-toml/) for the metadata format.

## Interpreter selection

For ordinary Python, Rivet checks these locations in order:

1. The `python` command or path in `.rivet.local.json`.
2. The project's `.venv/bin/python`.
3. An activated environment's `$VIRTUAL_ENV/bin/python`.
4. Available `python3.14`, `python3.13`, `python3.12`, `python3.11`, then `python3` commands.

The selected interpreter must meet the project's Python requirement. Local options can also select the application module and test runner:

```json
{
  "python": "/path/to/environment/bin/python",
  "python_module": "analysis_demo",
  "test_runner": "unittest"
}
```

Configure reports the interpreter version when an environment or explicit interpreter is already selected; otherwise it creates `.venv`. Run and Test do not invoke Configure.

## Tasks

| Task | Operation |
| --- | --- |
| Configure | Reuse/create an environment; uv mode runs an explicit sync |
| Check / Build | `compileall` over `src/` if present, otherwise the project root |
| Test | `unittest discover` under `tests/`, or selected `pytest` over the project |
| Run | Selected `python_module`, or the generated application module |
| Format / Lint | Ruff format/check in the selected environment |
| Package | The `build` module, or `uv build`, to build distributions |

Ruff, pytest, and the `build` module must be installed when their tasks are used. Doctor reports missing tools. Python traceback and syntax-error locations populate quickfix. Debug and Release use the same Python operations.

## uv

A new project selects uv with `--manager uv`; an external project selects it when `uv.lock` is present. Configure runs `uv sync --inexact --no-python-downloads`. It may install declared dependencies and build requirements, while retaining unrelated installed packages. An explicit `python` option becomes `--python` during sync.

Later Python tasks use the uv project environment through `uv run --no-sync`. Run Configure before first use and after dependency changes. uv's [locking and syncing reference](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/projects/sync/) describes these flags.

## Files and existing projects

`:RivetFile` adds a module under `src/<name>/` for a bare name, or at an explicit project-relative path. Import it from your code where needed. Additional console entry points belong in `pyproject.toml`.

An existing `pyproject.toml` is enough for discovery. External projects retain their import setup; select `python_module` and an appropriate environment explicitly. Use uv or your existing package manager for dependencies and lockfiles.
